---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Jochanni Labs — Decision Governance Research"
  description="Jochanni Labs is the research organization developing the framework for Decision Governance and runtime authority in AI-influenced enterprise execution. Founded by Kevin Moore. Publisher of the DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series."
>

  <!-- ── Page Header ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="jl-header">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="jl-header-label">Research Organization</p>
      <h1 class="jl-header-name">Jochanni Labs</h1>
      <p class="jl-header-sub">
        Focused on Decision Governance, runtime authority,
        and AI influenced enterprise execution.
      </p>
      <div class="jl-header-attribution">
        <span>Founded by Kevin Moore</span>
        <span class="jl-header-sep">·</span>
        <span>Publisher of the DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <main>

    <!-- ── Section 01 — Why the Labs Exists ──────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="jl-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="jl-section-head">
          <p class="jl-marker">01 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Institution</p>
          <h2 class="jl-title">Why Jochanni Labs Exists</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-two-col">
          <div class="jl-col">
            <p class="jl-prose">
              AI systems are participating in consequential decisions across enterprise
              operations. They classify risk, recommend exceptions, route customers,
              generate contracts, approve actions. The organizational disciplines
              required to govern that participation — to determine whether a decision
              path shaped by AI was authorized before consequence occurred — do not
              yet exist in most enterprises.
            </p>
            <p class="jl-prose">
              Jochanni Labs exists to build those disciplines. Not as a product.
              Not as a consulting service. As research — definitional work that
              establishes the vocabulary, the architecture, and the operational
              requirements of a governance category that is currently unnamed in
              most organizations and unaddressed in most governance frameworks.
            </p>
            <p class="jl-prose">
              The category is Decision Governance. The governing structure it requires
              is runtime authority. The framework being built to define it is DAL-X.
              These are not separate initiatives — they are the same project at
              different levels of abstraction, all developed and published through
              Jochanni Labs.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="jl-identity-card">
            <div class="jl-identity-row">
              <span class="jl-identity-label">Type</span>
              <span class="jl-identity-val">Research organization</span>
            </div>
            <div class="jl-identity-row">
              <span class="jl-identity-label">Founded</span>
              <span class="jl-identity-val">Kevin Moore</span>
            </div>
            <div class="jl-identity-row">
              <span class="jl-identity-label">Focus</span>
              <span class="jl-identity-val">Decision Governance · Runtime Authority</span>
            </div>
            <div class="jl-identity-row">
              <span class="jl-identity-label">Output</span>
              <span class="jl-identity-val">DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series</span>
            </div>
            <div class="jl-identity-row">
              <span class="jl-identity-label">Access</span>
              <span class="jl-identity-val">Open — no registration required</span>
            </div>
            <div class="jl-identity-sep"></div>
            <div class="jl-identity-not">
              <p class="jl-identity-not-label">Not a</p>
              <ul class="jl-identity-not-list">
                <li>Product company</li>
                <li>Consulting firm</li>
                <li>AI vendor</li>
                <li>Standards body</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 02 — The Strategic Intelligence Series ────────────── -->
    <section class="jl-section jl-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="jl-section-head">
          <p class="jl-marker">02 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Publication</p>
          <h2 class="jl-title">The Strategic Intelligence Series</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-two-col jl-two-col--flip">

          <div class="jl-series-block">
            <p class="jl-series-name">DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series</p>
            <p class="jl-series-tagline">
              Twenty briefings building the complete Decision Governance
              framework — from first principles through deployment reality
              and category defense.
            </p>
            <div class="jl-series-facts">
              <div class="jl-series-fact">
                <span class="jl-series-fact-num">20</span>
                <span class="jl-series-fact-label">Briefings</span>
              </div>
              <div class="jl-series-fact">
                <span class="jl-series-fact-num">4</span>
                <span class="jl-series-fact-label">Phases</span>
              </div>
              <div class="jl-series-fact">
                <span class="jl-series-fact-num">~80k</span>
                <span class="jl-series-fact-label">Words</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/briefings" class="jl-series-link">Read the Series →</a>
          </div>

          <div class="jl-col">
            <p class="jl-prose">
              The DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series is the primary output of
              Jochanni Labs — the vehicle through which Decision Governance is being
              defined in public. Twenty briefings, organized across four phases,
              each one a substantive piece of framework development. Not commentary.
              Not news. Not advisory content.
            </p>
            <p class="jl-prose">
              The Series is authored by Kevin Moore. It is published open access —
              no subscriptions, no accounts, no registration. The decision to publish
              openly is deliberate: a discipline cannot be defined privately. The
              vocabulary, concepts, and operational requirements of Decision Governance
              need to be available before organizations begin filling the category
              with insufficient substitutes. The Series is how that vocabulary enters
              the field.
            </p>
            <p class="jl-prose">
              The four phases build a complete framework. Phase 1 establishes Decision
              Governance as a distinct discipline, separating it from model governance,
              risk management, and compliance preparation. Phase 2 specifies the
              operational architecture — trigger logic, authority routing, execution
              gating, decision recording, audit evidence. Phase 3 addresses deployment
              reality. Phase 4 defends the category and establishes why runtime
              authority becomes unavoidable as agentic AI enters real workflows.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 03 — DAL-X ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="jl-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="jl-section-head">
          <p class="jl-marker">03 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Framework</p>
          <h2 class="jl-title">How DAL-X Fits</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-col jl-col--wide">
          <p class="jl-prose">
            DAL-X — the Decision Authority Layer — is the framework being developed
            by Jochanni Labs for the runtime authority discipline. It is not a product.
            It is the definitional framework that specifies what runtime authority
            is, what it requires structurally, and how organizations can build the
            authority infrastructure that makes AI-influenced execution governable.
          </p>
          <p class="jl-prose">
            The relationship between Jochanni Labs, the Series, and DAL-X is
            straightforward: the Labs is the institution, the Series is the research
            publication, and DAL-X is the framework the Series is constructing.
            The Series names DAL-X, defines its components, argues for its necessity,
            and defends it against alternative framings that would reduce Decision
            Governance to something already familiar and already insufficient.
          </p>
          <p class="jl-prose">
            DAL-X covers five operational mechanisms: trigger logic, which converts
            AI participation into a governance event; authority routing, which maps
            the decision path to the correct principal; execution gating, which
            enforces scope, conformance, and acknowledgment simultaneously; decision
            recording, which captures the authority record before consequence; and
            audit evidence, which provides the durable proof that governance was
            active — not retrospective.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-framework-row">
          <div class="jl-framework-item">
            <p class="jl-framework-item-name">Trigger Logic</p>
            <p class="jl-framework-item-desc">Converts AI participation into a governance event before the decision path moves forward</p>
          </div>
          <div class="jl-framework-item">
            <p class="jl-framework-item-name">Authority Routing</p>
            <p class="jl-framework-item-desc">Maps the decision path to the principal whose authority threshold matches the consequence level</p>
          </div>
          <div class="jl-framework-item">
            <p class="jl-framework-item-name">Execution Gating</p>
            <p class="jl-framework-item-desc">Enforces scope, conformance, and acknowledgment simultaneously before execution proceeds</p>
          </div>
          <div class="jl-framework-item">
            <p class="jl-framework-item-name">Decision Recording</p>
            <p class="jl-framework-item-desc">Captures the authority record before consequence — not after, when governance can only explain, not prove</p>
          </div>
          <div class="jl-framework-item">
            <p class="jl-framework-item-name">Audit Evidence</p>
            <p class="jl-framework-item-desc">Provides the durable proof chain that governance was active at the execution point</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-dal-x-cta">
          <a href="/dal-x" class="btn-outline">Full DAL-X Framework →</a>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 04 — Why Runtime Authority Matters ────────────────── -->
    <section class="jl-section jl-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="jl-section-head">
          <p class="jl-marker">04 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Foundation</p>
          <h2 class="jl-title">Why Runtime Authority Matters</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-thesis">
          <p class="jl-thesis-text">
            Policy without runtime enforcement is intent, not governance.
            The difference between an organization that believes it governs
            AI-influenced work and one that can prove it is the presence of
            an authority layer active at the execution point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-col jl-col--wide">
          <p class="jl-prose">
            The governance investments most enterprises have made in AI concentrate
            on visibility: system inventories, risk classifications, policy libraries,
            use case reviews, monitoring dashboards. These investments are useful.
            They tell an organization where AI exists, what risks have been reviewed,
            and what standards apply in theory. They do not tell an organization
            whether the specific decision path shaped by AI was authorized before
            it moved toward consequence.
          </p>
          <p class="jl-prose">
            Runtime authority is the active governing structure that answers that
            question before it becomes unanswerable. It operates before execution,
            not after. It determines whether the chain of AI participation,
            recommendation, classification, routing, and human action had the
            standing to proceed. Post-execution audit can explain what happened.
            Only runtime authority can govern what is allowed to happen.
          </p>
          <p class="jl-prose">
            Enterprises operating AI systems without runtime authority are not
            in a neutral position. They are accumulating exposure that grows with
            each AI-influenced decision that cannot be proven to have been
            authorized at the moment it occurred. That exposure is invisible
            until it is material — and by the time it is material, the governance
            infrastructure required to address it cannot be built retroactively.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 05 — Institutional Position ───────────────────────── -->
    <section class="jl-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="jl-section-head">
          <p class="jl-marker">05 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Position</p>
          <h2 class="jl-title">What Jochanni Labs Is and Is Not</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="jl-position-grid">

          <div class="jl-position-col jl-position-col--is">
            <p class="jl-position-col-label">Jochanni Labs is</p>
            <ul class="jl-position-list">
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">A research organization</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">Its output is frameworks, analysis, and publication — not products, not advisory engagements.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">Category-originating</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">Decision Governance is a discipline being defined here, not adopted from an existing category.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">Publication-led</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">The DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series is the primary vehicle. Research is shared openly, not licensed.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">Institutionally independent</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">No vendor relationships, no advisory boards, no product roadmap that the research must serve.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="jl-position-divider"></div>

          <div class="jl-position-col jl-position-col--isnot">
            <p class="jl-position-col-label jl-position-col-label--dim">Jochanni Labs is not</p>
            <ul class="jl-position-list">
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">A product company</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">DAL-X is a framework. It is not software, a platform, or a tool that Jochanni Labs sells.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">A consulting firm</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">The Labs does not provide implementation services, advisory retainers, or governance audits.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">A broad AI company</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">The scope is narrow by design. Decision Governance. Runtime authority. AI-influenced execution. Nothing else.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="jl-position-item-head">A standards body</span>
                <span class="jl-position-item-body">The Labs sets no compliance requirements. The research is available to organizations to apply as the discipline develops.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Footer CTA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="jl-footer-cta">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="jl-footer-cta-label">Jochanni Labs · DAL-X Strategic Intelligence Series</p>
        <p class="jl-footer-cta-text">
          Twenty briefings defining Decision Governance as the runtime authority
          discipline for AI-influenced enterprise execution. Open access. No registration.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a href="/briefings" class="btn-primary">Read All 20 Briefings</a>
          <a href="/about" class="btn-outline">Kevin Moore</a>
          <a href="/dal-x" class="btn-outline">DAL-X Framework</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </main>
</Base>
